    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>No, coef returns the item parameters. It has nothing to do with theta estimates.</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>can we use regression or correlations between  <img alt=\"{\\theta}\" src=\"https://ci3.googleusercontent.com/proxy/9DoWfaVFnUYqYY1Oyr00Qc1CE32dxskah3_6fLPntaaLUuUT4B8dpsCov01K5FI_gVZq38yKqu8Kkv1u3iTuZBs7TJQjU5XHXoQlRNQ7pzKE5F-bUyX8OeqnSxhM4A=s0-d-e1-ft#http://upload.wikimedia.org/math/6/0/8/6083a03bd0bb5a107b32a5faec942099.png\"> in order to test our hypothesis?<br></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>You can, but you shouldn&#39;t. You&#39;ll experience shrinkage effects due to factor score prediction. The better way to do this is via latent regression methods, but they are currently only available on the development version (or if you are using Rasch models, this is possible in mixedmirt already). </div><div><br></div><div>Phil</div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
